Served as a Teaching Assistant, collaborating faculty and course managers during the 2020 transition to online learning for a climate science course targeted toward non-STEM students
 
<a href='https://registrar.princeton.edu/course-offerings/course-details?courseid=011153&term=1272'>Climate: Past, Present, and Future (GEO-102)</a>

## Responsibilities included:
* Collaborating with faculty and course managers in the transition to online learning by reviewing and modifying new online materials (e.g. exams and quizzes), primarily implemented through Canvas, for a 150+ student course
* Grading and providing feedback on exams and homework assignments
* Hosting weekly Zoom office hours and additional supplemental meetings for undergraduate students

## Course description:
Are human activities changing our climate? Is climate change a major problem? To answer these questions, we will learn the basics of how Earth's climate works and how climate shapes environments. We will also investigate past climate changes and identify the most important ways in which climate change can affect the natural world and humans. Finally, we will use climate science to evaluate possible responses and solutions. The course is designed for students from all majors--no science background required--while still offering a comprehensive overview for students seeking to pursue climate-related issues.
